# Ch270 Amyloidosis and Other Restrictive Cardiomyopathies

## Restrictive Cardiomyopathy

- **Definition** - cardiomyopathy dominated by abnormal diastolic function within a non-compliant ventricle, often a/w mildly decreased contractility and ejection fraction
- **Natural Hx of restrictive cardiomyopathy:**
  - Initial SOBOE and exercise intolerance is mild and not noticed by the patients
  - Generally presents initially w/ generalised congestive Sx, esp. systemic congestion due to more prominant right-sided Sx (despite filling pressures elevated in both ventricles)
  - ?This may be due to a reduced RV output such that LV pre-load is limited
  - Cardiac output is maintained and only declines late in the disease course
- **Etiology of restrictive cardiomyopathy** - most causes are "infiltrative" (usually amyloidosis):
  - **Infiltrative disease** - infiltration of abnormal substances between monocytes:
    - <u>Acquired amyloidosis</u> - AL amyloidosis, ATTRwt amyloidosis (senile systemic amyloidosis)
    - <u>Hereditary amyloidosis</u> - most commonly ATTRv amyloidosis
  - **Storage disease** - storage of abnormal substances within monocytes:
    - <u>Haemochromatosis</u> - primary haemochromatosis, secondary haemochromatosis
    - <u>Inherited metabolic defects</u> - Fabry's disease, glycogen storage disease (II, III)
  - **Miscellaneous:**
    - <u>Genetics</u> - desminopathy (a/w skeletal muscle involvement), genetic variants more commonly a/w DCM/ HCM (forme fruste of HCM) presenting phenotypically as RCM
    - <u>Fibrotic reactions</u> - radiation, scleroderma, hypereosinophilic syndrome

  
  ![](../img/causes_of_rcm.png)
- **DDx of predominantly RHF:**
  - RCM
  - Constrictive pericarditis

